[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Test-Driven Development (TDD) in Core Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Test-Driven Development (TDD) is a software development process that relies on the repetition of a very short development cycle: first the developer writes an (initially failing) automated test case that defines a desired improvement or new function, then produces the minimum amount of code to pass that test, and finally refactors the new code to acceptable standards. Kent Beck, who is credited with having developed or rediscovered the technique, introduced it as part of Extreme Programming."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The TDD Cycle: Red-Green-Refactor"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"TDD follows a simple, repeatable cycle, often referred to as Red-Green-Refactor:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Red:** Write a failing test. This ensures that the test actually tests something and that you understand the requirements."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Green:** Write the minimal amount of code necessary to pass the test. Focus on getting the test to pass, not on perfect code."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Refactor:** Clean up the code, improve its structure, and remove duplication while ensuring that all tests still pass. This is where you make the code maintainable and readable."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of TDD"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Higher Code Quality:** TDD leads to more modular, flexible, and extensible code."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Reduced Debugging Time:** Because you're constantly testing, you catch bugs earlier in the development process."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Clearer Requirements:** Writing tests first forces you to think about the requirements more clearly."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Living Documentation:** Tests serve as executable documentation of how the code is supposed to work."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Implementing a Simple String Calculator"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's illustrate TDD with a simple example: a String Calculator that adds numbers from a string. We'll use JUnit for testing."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Set up your Project"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new Java project and add JUnit as a dependency. If you are using Maven, add the following to your pom.xml:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"<dependency>
    <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
    <artifactId>junit-jupiter-api</artifactId>
    <version>5.10.0</version>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Red - Write a Failing Test"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a test class `StringCalculatorTest` and write a test case for an empty string. This should fail because the `add` method doesn't exist yet."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

public class StringCalculatorTest {

    @Test
    void testEmptyString() {
        StringCalculator calculator = new StringCalculator();
        assertEquals(0, calculator.add(""));
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Green - Make the Test Pass"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create the `StringCalculator` class with an `add` method that returns 0 for an empty string."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"public class StringCalculator {
    public int add(String numbers) {
        return 0;
    }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the test. It should now pass."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Refactor - Clean Up (if needed)"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this simple case, there's not much to refactor. The code is already clean and concise."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 5: Red - Write Another Failing Test"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, let's add a test case for a single number."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

public class StringCalculatorTest {

    @Test
    void testEmptyString() {
        StringCalculator calculator = new StringCalculator();
        assertEquals(0, calculator.add(""));
    }

    @Test
    void testSingleNumber() {
        StringCalculator calculator = new StringCalculator();
        assertEquals(1, calculator.add("1"));
    }
}"},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 6: Green - Make the Test Pass"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modify the `add` method to handle a single number."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"public class StringCalculator {
    public int add(String numbers) {
        if (numbers.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        } else {
            return Integer.parseInt(numbers);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the tests. Both should now pass."},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 7: Refactor - Clean Up (if needed)"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"Again, the code is relatively simple, so minimal refactoring is needed."},{"order":36,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 8: Red - Write Another Failing Test"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"Add a test case for two numbers separated by a comma."},{"order":38,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

public class StringCalculatorTest {

    @Test
    void testEmptyString() {
        StringCalculator calculator = new StringCalculator();
        assertEquals(0, calculator.add(""));
    }

    @Test
    void testSingleNumber() {
        StringCalculator calculator = new StringCalculator();
        assertEquals(1, calculator.add("1"));
    }

    @Test
    void testTwoNumbers() {
        StringCalculator calculator = new StringCalculator();
        assertEquals(3, calculator.add("1,2"));
    }
}"},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 9: Green - Make the Test Pass"},{"order":40,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Arrays;

public class StringCalculator {
    public int add(String numbers) {
        if (numbers.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        } else {
            return Arrays.stream(numbers.split(","))
                         .mapToInt(Integer::parseInt)
                         .sum();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the tests. All should now pass."},{"order":42,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 10: Refactor - Clean Up"},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's refactor to extract the parsing logic into a separate method."},{"order":44,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Arrays;

public class StringCalculator {
    public int add(String numbers) {
        if (numbers.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        } else {
            return sum(splitNumbers(numbers));
        }
    }

    private int[] splitNumbers(String numbers) {
        return Arrays.stream(numbers.split(","))
                     .mapToInt(Integer::parseInt)
                     .toArray();
    }

    private int sum(int[] numbers) {
        return Arrays.stream(numbers).sum();
    }
}"},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the tests again to ensure the refactoring didn't break anything."},{"order":46,"type":"subheading","text":"Continuing the TDD Cycle"},{"order":47,"type":"paragraph","text":"You would continue this Red-Green-Refactor cycle, adding more test cases for different scenarios (e.g., handling new lines as delimiters, negative numbers, numbers greater than 1000) and implementing the corresponding logic."},{"order":48,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":49,"type":"paragraph","text":"TDD is a powerful technique for writing high-quality, maintainable code. By writing tests first, you ensure that your code meets the requirements and is easy to test and debug. While it might seem slower at first, TDD can save you time and effort in the long run by reducing bugs and improving code quality."}]
